+### Get an access token without signing in again
+
+Access tokens expire after about an hour. Call `authorize(...)` to get a fresh access token for the `scopes` given to `initialize(...)` without showing the sign-in sheet again. Once the user has granted the scopes, no UI is shown. With `interactive: false`, the call never shows any UI and rejects with the `NEEDS_INTERACTION` error code when the user must consent first, which makes it safe to call from a background task. This follows Google's guidance to [separate authorization from authentication](https://developers.google.com/identity/authorization/android):
+
+```typescript
+import { ErrorCode, GoogleSignIn } from '@capawesome/capacitor-google-sign-in';
+
+const authorize = async () => {
+  try {
+    const { accessToken } = await GoogleSignIn.authorize({
+      interactive: false,
+    });
+    console.log(accessToken);
+  } catch (error) {
+    if (error.code === ErrorCode.NeedsInteraction) {
+      // Ask the user to grant the scopes, e.g. after a tap on a button:
+      const { accessToken } = await GoogleSignIn.authorize({
+        interactive: true,
+      });
+      console.log(accessToken);
+    } else {
+      console.log('Another error occurred:', error);
+    }
+  }
+};
+```
+
+On Web, a token cannot be obtained without a redirect, so `interactive: false` always rejects with `NEEDS_INTERACTION`, and `interactive: true` redirects as `signIn()` does.
+
 ### Complete the sign-in flow on the Web
 
 On Web, the app is redirected back to the `redirectUrl` after the user signs in. Call `handleRedirectCallback()` there to exchange the authorization code for tokens and complete the sign-in flow. Only available on Web:
